{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2416 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "9ed20fb3c6344f7d4a135490ed1652d348415dd31c27939ea33e145c893ace02" :edb/db-before-t 2415 :edb/db-after-t 2416 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535728 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:05:23.459Z" 13194139535728 true] [17592186171947 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363530 13194139535728 true] [17592186171948 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363551 13194139535728 true] [17592186171949 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363537 13194139535728 true] [17592186171949 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363544 13194139535728 true] [17592186172498 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535728 true] [17592186172498 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/2f577535c7e293a64139c0c68d2f09ea083041d806a634ae2d9a27055e96c00d.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x+y+1$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x-1}$" 13194139535728 true] [17592186172498 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/q-50350.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = x + y + 1$\n2. $y^{′} = \\frac{y}{x + 1}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{y}{x - 1}$" 13194139535728 false] [17592186172498 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/b8a9560870e28c751b8869e298ffabdbcc23eec720c7a1057d55032a62767183.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x+y+1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ but \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}\\to \\frac{y}{2}<\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x-1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ and \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=|\\frac{y}{x-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363530 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363531 13194139535728 true] [17592186363530 1089 "q-120286" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363530 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535728 true] [17592186363530 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/e8b573c6c2c59123de46346ccfc717fa89a98f7838e643a5e82972d753383588.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363530 1113 #uuid "300b5b75-ac3e-5a5e-8fbd-21f5c2d703aa" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363530 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/2f654bc96d27ac53dd501b10c5beb0d1c7d04ff4f643c5044692afac26ca2ba8.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0$ only.\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$ only, and for $y\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363532 13194139535728 true] [17592186363531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363533 13194139535728 true] [17592186363531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363534 13194139535728 true] [17592186363531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363535 13194139535728 true] [17592186363531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363536 13194139535728 true] [17592186363531 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535728 true] [17592186363531 1041 "selection" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363531 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363533 13194139535728 true] [17592186363531 1110 #uuid "f861bdfb-15c7-5413-b532-1ac602d9805a" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363532 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363532 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535728 true] [17592186363532 1111 #uuid "e23fa630-a3b0-52d3-853d-521ef5281c3a" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363533 1034 "I only" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535728 true] [17592186363533 1111 #uuid "41934154-1ca0-5719-a3b4-a7b2b9c1cc4c" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363534 1034 "III only" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363534 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535728 true] [17592186363534 1111 #uuid "3d46a061-d0f7-5c63-a052-9c2b6f303086" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363535 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363535 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535728 true] [17592186363535 1111 #uuid "29196c44-9eaf-5de2-9449-15e09be3138d" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363536 1034 "II only" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363536 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535728 true] [17592186363536 1111 #uuid "49483c01-dabe-5d9e-8609-577b6d11e377" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363537 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363538 13194139535728 true] [17592186363537 1089 "q-50135" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363537 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535728 true] [17592186363537 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/65b74168f1165014df494c238dc400ab772f2c2486935ba6b78fdcbc6658b3b6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363537 1113 #uuid "21d8bdd9-00f5-5d9f-b814-9441e0613ff1" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363537 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/3b77cdfa4958cd1892c31baf858b5f8cbe29e00e8e2a45b698a43c5ddae6198e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the line $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on this line.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the line $y=0$ divide the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y.\n$$" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363539 13194139535728 true] [17592186363538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363540 13194139535728 true] [17592186363538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363541 13194139535728 true] [17592186363538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363542 13194139535728 true] [17592186363538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363543 13194139535728 true] [17592186363538 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535728 true] [17592186363538 1041 "selection" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363538 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363541 13194139535728 true] [17592186363538 1110 #uuid "5a49e3d5-80d5-5beb-8664-852d8076eee6" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363539 1034 "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363539 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363539 1111 #uuid "623f5543-b4f4-535e-bc10-13dce009acfc" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363540 1034 "{y}^{′}=x" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363540 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363540 1111 #uuid "5e33182a-aec5-575b-96ca-d5d58fd6d6f3" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363541 1034 "{y}^{′}=y" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363541 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363541 1111 #uuid "6df3547a-313b-5d3c-a273-bbbff051fd3f" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363542 1034 "{y}^{′}={e}^{xy}" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363542 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363542 1111 #uuid "c0529f61-0ba3-505d-b0f7-3ebeb5477b0d" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363543 1034 "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363543 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363543 1111 #uuid "ef8a33b7-344f-5788-8736-5604a7d25895" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363544 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363545 13194139535728 true] [17592186363544 1089 "q-50188" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363544 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535728 true] [17592186363544 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/09ba55ca21ef70904948bcf6d9c0787bee8f7836a233e0d7ce689549966120cf.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363544 1113 #uuid "3d30b528-f410-51c6-84f9-1799c3e31ee0" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363544 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/0052e47a810dc6c82dd68ff9d0527a0b121f935d42067e063478f4f98817ffed.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363546 13194139535728 true] [17592186363545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363547 13194139535728 true] [17592186363545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363548 13194139535728 true] [17592186363545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363549 13194139535728 true] [17592186363545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363550 13194139535728 true] [17592186363545 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535728 true] [17592186363545 1041 "selection" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363545 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363546 13194139535728 true] [17592186363545 1110 #uuid "5125d1f0-df69-5101-a6ab-329f67d3df8e" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363546 1034 "{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363546 1111 #uuid "0d778715-435c-5af7-a61a-762082a2c2b1" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363547 1034 "{y}^{′}=(x+1)y" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363547 1111 #uuid "f5d16394-e87f-56fd-ad43-02845985acf9" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363548 1034 "{y}^{′}=xy" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363548 1111 #uuid "0eaf23b5-895f-56f9-a72e-8bdf564d18dc" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363549 1034 "{y}^{′}=x+y" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363549 1111 #uuid "b128b61a-3671-5aad-8b0b-b33bd3621e7c" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363550 1034 "{y}^{′}=x-y" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363550 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363550 1111 #uuid "0f5e9e8f-6a4d-5a7f-90af-b25b07cbe45c" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363551 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363552 13194139535728 true] [17592186363551 1089 "q-80703" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363551 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535728 true] [17592186363551 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/da6318e1b1b8123845993da64cea95f640b0cb67ad94bb183bbbe9e5b1bd7b89.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363551 1113 #uuid "8e9fbf1d-5efa-58cb-a6e1-75538211843e" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363551 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/8f8ffabcc5b03b1f1b5856f4eb187137d1bf84043e47a43f6777df7b267853ec.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy-4x=x(y-4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(y-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=4$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>4$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<4$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-4x=x(y-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=xy-4x.$" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363553 13194139535728 true] [17592186363552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363554 13194139535728 true] [17592186363552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363555 13194139535728 true] [17592186363552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363556 13194139535728 true] [17592186363552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363557 13194139535728 true] [17592186363552 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535728 true] [17592186363552 1041 "selection" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363552 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363554 13194139535728 true] [17592186363552 1110 #uuid "fd99a06d-d546-5826-a582-a0212f6e7f61" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363553 1034 "{y}^{′}=(y-4)^{2}" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363553 1111 #uuid "f012c57e-b5e7-5560-9b2d-80d38c5a8a56" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363554 1034 "{y}^{′}=xy-4x" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363554 1111 #uuid "a430c54e-9a5a-520c-8ec1-2a84dc75d7cd" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363555 1034 "{y}^{′}=x-4y" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363555 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363555 1111 #uuid "8a520fac-e268-5245-b9a5-3e76ac57955b" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363556 1034 "{y}^{′}=xy" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363556 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535728 true] [17592186363556 1111 #uuid "2c379474-7aaf-57e2-b86d-66a4e768a01f" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363557 1034 "{y}^{′}=x(y-4)^{2}" 13194139535728 true] [17592186363557 1103 #edb/ref 17592186